BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:christmas-day@todaycelebrates.com
DTSTAMP:20261014T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261225
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261226
SUMMARY:Christmas Day
RRULE:FREQ=YEARLY
DESCRIPTION:Christmas Day falls on December 25 and is at once a Christian f
 east marking the birth of Jesus and the largest secular holiday on the Ame
 rican calendar. Almost nothing about how Americans keep it is as old as it
  feels: the date was settled by argument rather than record\, the tree rea
 ched the English-speaking world from Germany by way of the British court\,
  and the Santa Claus on the wrapping paper was assembled over fifty-eight 
 years by a New York poet\, a political cartoonist\, and an advertising art
 ist.\n\nhttps://todaycelebrates.com/holiday/christmas-day/
URL;VALUE=URI:https://todaycelebrates.com/holiday/christmas-day/
CATEGORIES:Celebration
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
CLASS:PUBLIC
SEQUENCE:0
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:christmas-eve@todaycelebrates.com
DTSTAMP:20261014T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261224
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261225
SUMMARY:Christmas Eve
RRULE:FREQ=YEARLY
DESCRIPTION:Christmas Eve is December 24\, and in much of the English-speak
 ing world it carries more of the holiday's weight than Christmas Day itsel
 f: the arrival\, the service\, the last errand\, the night something is su
 pposed to happen. It is not a federal holiday in the United States and nev
 er has been — when federal offices close on the 24th\, it is because a p
 resident signed an order for that year. Most of what Americans actually do
  on the night can be dated\, and it is newer than it feels.\n\nhttps://tod
 aycelebrates.com/holiday/christmas-eve/
URL;VALUE=URI:https://todaycelebrates.com/holiday/christmas-eve/
CATEGORIES:Celebration
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
CLASS:PUBLIC
SEQUENCE:0
END:VEVENT
