UID:new-years-day@todaycelebrates.com
DTSTAMP:20261014T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20270101
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20270102
SUMMARY:New Year's Day
RRULE:FREQ=YEARLY
DESCRIPTION:New Year's Day falls on January 1\, which is an administrative 
 decision rather than a fact of nature. The date has been moved\, abandoned
 \, and restored more than once in Western Europe — Britain and its Ameri
 can colonies did not adopt it until 1752 — and most of what Americans ac
 tually do on the day\, from the parade in Pasadena to the ball in Times Sq
 uare\, was invented between 1788 and 1907.\n\nhttps://todaycelebrates.com/
 holiday/new-years-day/
URL;VALUE=URI:https://todaycelebrates.com/holiday/new-years-day/
CATEGORIES:Celebration
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
CLASS:PUBLIC
SEQUENCE:0
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:new-years-eve@todaycelebrates.com
DTSTAMP:20261014T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261231
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20270101
SUMMARY:New Year's Eve
RRULE:FREQ=YEARLY
DESCRIPTION:New Year's Eve is December 31\, and in the United States it hol
 ds two very different midnights. One is a commercial spectacle a newspaper
  owner invented in 1904 to publicize his new building. The other is a vigi
 l that Black congregations have kept since the night of December 31\, 1862
 \, when people waited in the dark for the Emancipation Proclamation to tak
 e effect.\n\nhttps://todaycelebrates.com/holiday/new-years-eve/
URL;VALUE=URI:https://todaycelebrates.com/holiday/new-years-eve/
CATEGORIES:Celebration
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
CLASS:PUBLIC
SEQUENCE:0
END:VEVENT
